      "@type": "FAQPage",
      "mainEntity": [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is NurosOS?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "NurosOS is an open-source experimental substrate for synthetic development, written in Rust and Python. It provides the runtime, interfaces, environments, developmental mechanisms, observability, and reproducibility infrastructure required to instantiate, develop, measure, fork, replay, and compare artificial cognitive systems. The fundamental object is not MODEL but TRAJECTORY; not AGENT but DEVELOPING ORGANISM."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How does NurosOS differ from an AI agent framework?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Traditional AI follows the inversion Model -> Training -> Agent. NurosOS inverts this again: Developmental Genome -> Environment -> Experience -> Development -> Individual Cognitive Trajectory -> Artificial Organism. The agent is not the primitive; the developing organism is the primitive. NurosOS provides developmental state, artificial ontogenesis, environmental interaction, mind provenance, mind diff, reproducibility, and developmental experiments — none of which are primitives of an agent framework."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the Same Genome / Different World experiment?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The flagship NurosOS experiment instantiates two organisms from the same developmental genome, places them in differently-seeded environments, develops both for the same number of steps, and measures the developmental divergence between them. The experiment demonstrates Computational Developmental Divergence: identical initial computational conditions producing divergent developmental states under different environmental histories. This is an observable computational fact, NOT evidence of consciousness or biological individuality."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does NurosOS implement consciousness?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "No. NurosOS does not implement consciousness, does not create biological life, and does not solve artificial consciousness. The divergence measured by the flagship experiment is an observable computational fact, not evidence of subjective experience. NurosOS treats consciousness, sentience, subjective experience, and artificial life as open research questions, not as solved problems."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is NurosOS production-ready?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "No. NurosOS is research software at v0.3.0-alpha. It is not production-ready. The MinimumOrganism cognitive engine is intentionally a deterministic epsilon-greedy policy with hand-coded heuristic biases — not an LLM, not a neural network. This is by design for reproducibility and for isolating the effect of environment from the effect of engine."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What programming languages does NurosOS use?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Rust (>=1.75) for the developmental substrate (nuros-dev crate, exposed via PyO3) and the neuromorphic kernel (preserved from v0.1.0 as a future execution backend). Python (>=3.10) for the cognitive layer, experiments, and integration tests. The flagship experiment runner (experiments/same_genome_different_world.py) is Python; the developmental primitives it invokes are Rust."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is a Developmental Genome?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A DevelopmentalGenome is a serializable, hashable specification of an organism's initial developmental conditions. It includes architecture, initial memory, initial capabilities, plasticity rules, biases, maturation schedule, homeostasis setpoints, energy model, and mutation parameters. Two organisms instantiated from the same genome, placed in different environments, should produce divergent developmental trajectories. The genome has a canonical SHA-256 hash that becomes part of every checkpoint, trajectory, and manifest."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is Mind Diff?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "MindDiff is a structured comparison of two organism states across memory, self-model, values (action preferences), capabilities, prediction, behavior, and developmental state. Inspired conceptually by version-control diffs, but designed for cognitive state. The output is machine-readable JSON with a human-readable rendering. It supports diff(mind_A, mind_B) and diff(mind_t1, mind_t2)."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How is reproducibility guaranteed?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Every experiment produces a ReproducibilityManifest recording: mind_id, genome_hash, runtime_hash, environment_hash, experiment_hash, random_seed, environment_seed, checkpoint_hash, configuration_hash, dependency_versions, timestamp, n_steps, and limitations. Two runs with the same manifest values produce identical trajectories (verified by 322 tests: 247 Python HippoCore + 57 Rust + 18 integration). Replay fidelity is classified as EXACT, APPROXIMATE, or NON_REPRODUCIBLE."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the HippoCore integration?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The HippoCore integration (v0.4.0-alpha, PHASES 1-10) adds a MemoryEngine ABC with two implementations: DefaultMemoryContract (backward-compat) and HippoCoreMemory (episodic encoding, structured provenance, 5 replay policies, fast-to-slow consolidation, memory event emission for the developmental trajectory, and causal graph integration). Shipped as 10 logical phases on the feature/hippocore-integration-audit branch. 247 Python tests passing. NO LLM dependency."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does HippoCore depend on an LLM?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "No. The HippoCore package is fully local and deterministic. All novelty / importance / prediction_error weighting is computed from local memory fields. Pattern separation uses content-addressable storage (UUIDs + tags). Consolidation uses Jaccard / content-prefix similarity, not embedding-based clustering. The no-LLM-dependency invariant is satisfied (master prompt section 23)."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What are the 5 HippoCore replay policies?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "RecentReplayPolicy (most recent N), ImportanceWeightedReplayPolicy (proportional to importance squared), NoveltyWeightedReplayPolicy (proportional to 1/(1+access_count) - less-accessed is more novel), PredictionErrorWeightedReplayPolicy (proportional to absolute prediction_error - the surprise signal), RandomReplayPolicy (uniform baseline). All deterministic when seeded."
          }
        }
